# Copy to .env in this folder on the server, once. Owner root, mode 600, never in git.
# The guard keeps a second run from replacing a filled-in file (and its key pair):
#   [ -e .env ] || sudo install -m 600 -o root -g root .env.example .env
# Keep a copy of the filled-in file in the password manager.

# The API key and secret: every token the app makes for a call, every call the
# app makes to LiveKit's API and every webhook LiveKit sends are signed with
# them. Never typed: make-keys.sh makes them on the server, writes them here and
# into ../app/.env (as LIVEKIT_API_KEY and LIVEKIT_API_SECRET), and shows neither.
#   sudo ./make-keys.sh
# After they change, LiveKit has to be recreated to read them:
#   sudo docker compose up -d
LIVEKIT_API_KEY=
LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=

# Where LiveKit sends its webhooks (room started and finished, someone joined or
# left), signed with the key above: the app's public address and the route the
# calls feature adds, https://<the app's public name>/api/calls/livekit/webhook.
# They come back in through Caddy like any other caller's request; LiveKit has no
# other way to the app. While this is empty, make-keys.sh fills it in from
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL in ../app/.env. When the app's address moves, change it here
# too (the old name would answer with a redirect), then `sudo docker compose up -d`.
# To send no webhooks, comment the line out.
LIVEKIT_WEBHOOK_URL=

# The server's public IPv4 address, only if LiveKit gets it wrong by itself.
# Empty (the default): LiveKit asks a public STUN server at startup which
# address its packets leave from, which follows the server through a move. Set
# it only if `sudo docker compose logs livekit | grep -m1 -o '"nodeIP": "[^"]*"'`
# shows another address than the server's (../README.md step 23).
LIVEKIT_NODE_IP=
